<rss version="0.92"><channel><item>
            <title>[LU-21107] make clean doesn't remove kernel object files</title>
                <link>https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-21107</link>
                <project id="10000" key="LU">Lustre</project>
                    <description>&lt;p&gt;Since 31e5fc87aebf ("&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-18876" title="Implement copy-builtin for Lustre" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-18876"&gt;LU-18876&lt;/a&gt; build: remove MOSTLYCLEANFILES"), `make clean` hasn't removed *.ko files. This actually caused a concrete problem for me - since 3cf87a83a0fd ("&lt;a href="https://jira.whamcloud.com/browse/LU-18876" title="Implement copy-builtin for Lustre" class="issue-link" data-issue-key="LU-18876"&gt;LU-18876&lt;/a&gt; libcfs: move to its final place for upstreaming") moved the location of the libcfs.ko file, after I updated my lustre tree I ended up with 2 conflicting copies of the kernel module. This broke the llmount.sh script:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="code panel" style="border-width: 1px;"&gt;&lt;div class="codeContent panelContent"&gt;
&lt;pre class="code-java"&gt;
lustre-release $ git checkout 3cf87a83a0fd~1
lustre-release $ sh autogen.sh &amp;amp;&amp;amp; ./configure --disable-gss-keyring --disable-gss --enable-server --with-linux=/lib/modules/$(uname -r)/build
lustre-release &amp;amp; make -j8

lustre-release $ git checkout 3cf87a83a0fd
lustre-release $ make clean &amp;gt; /dev/&lt;span class="code-keyword"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt; &amp;amp;&amp;amp; make -j16 &amp;gt; /dev/&lt;span class="code-keyword"&gt;null&lt;/span&gt;
lustre-release $ find . -name libcfs.ko
./lnet/libcfs/libcfs.ko
./libcfs/libcfs/libcfs.ko

lustre-release $ sudo ./lustre/tests/llmount.sh

lustre-release $ dmesg | tail -3
[82650.146615] obdclass: Unknown symbol compat_totalram_pages (err 0)
[82650.146898] obdclass: disagrees about version of symbol llcrypt_sb_free
[82650.146912] obdclass: Unknown symbol llcrypt_sb_free (err -22&lt;/pre&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;p&gt; &lt;/p&gt;</description>
